fix(auto-nudge): never treat its own turn or an armed-mid-turn as idle

Two live misfires on an actively-driven thread, reconstructed from the
event journal:

1. Arming during a RUNNING turn recorded no baseline: the decider stored
   baselineSettledTurnId only when the latest turn was already completed.
   The operator armed at 10:04:52 while an 8-minute turn ran; when it
   completed at 10:05:49 the null baseline made it look like fresh idle
   work and Auto Nudge dispatched 516ms later — into a conversation the
   operator was actively driving. The baseline is now whatever turn the
   thread has at arming time, regardless of state.

2. The nudge's own turn completion re-armed the next dispatch: eligibility
   excluded only the completion that TRIGGERED the last dispatch, never
   the turn the dispatched message STARTED. Nudge #1's turn completed at
   10:07:23 and nudge #2 fired 411ms later citing it — a self-
   perpetuating paid loop bounded only by the round cap or a manual Stop.
   The config now records lastDispatchedMessageId; without background
   continuation, a completed turn started by that message is rejected at
   the server decider and never proposed by the foreground client.
   Background continuation keeps chaining — unattended multi-round
   progress is its purpose — still bounded by maxRounds.

The new contract field uses a decoding default so configurations
persisted before it decode unchanged. No timer, delay, or silence
threshold was added; dispatch authority remains exact-completion-driven.

Validation: 16/16 decider auto-nudge tests including three new
regressions (running-turn baseline, self-turn rejection, background
chaining preserved); 443 orchestration; 1,551 web unit; 54 auto-nudge
browser tests; TypeScript clean on contracts, server, and web.

